---
import TextbookCallout from '../TextbookCallout.astro';
import TextbookChunk from '../TextbookChunk.astro';
import TextbookPageIntro from '../TextbookPageIntro.astro';
import TextbookSectionBlock from '../TextbookSectionBlock.astro';
import type { TextbookChapterModel, TextbookSectionModel } from '../../../lib/textbook-model';

interface Props {
  chapter: TextbookChapterModel;
}

const { chapter } = Astro.props as Props;

const requireSection = (code: string): TextbookSectionModel => {
  const section = chapter.sections.find((candidate) => candidate.code === code);
  if (!section) {
    throw new Error(`Missing textbook section "${code}" for rebuilt chapter ${chapter.code}.`);
  }
  return section;
};

const a11 = requireSection('A1.1');
const a12 = requireSection('A1.2');
const a13 = requireSection('A1.3');
const a14 = requireSection('A1.4');
---

<TextbookPageIntro code={chapter.code} title={chapter.title} summary={chapter.summary} visibility={chapter.visibility}>
  <p>
    This chapter builds a system-level view of how computers store data, execute instructions, manage
    resources, and translate programs into executable behaviour. Read it as a connected model rather than
    as isolated definitions: the strongest exam answers explain how components interact and why one design
    choice is more suitable than another in a given context.
  </p>
  <TextbookCallout tone="note" label="Reading pattern">
    <p>
      Standard level students should focus on the core explanation in each section first. Higher level
      students should then use the labelled HL extensions to compare architectures, explain resource
      allocation issues, and evaluate translation choices with justified criteria.
    </p>
  </TextbookCallout>
</TextbookPageIntro>

<TextbookSectionBlock section={a11}>
  <TextbookChunk
    label="A1.1.1, A1.1.2, A1.1.4, A1.1.5"
    summary="Build a joined picture of CPU components, GPU purpose, primary memory, and the fetch-decode-execute cycle."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      A computer works because several components cooperate in a fixed sequence. The CPU controls the
      process, primary memory keeps instructions and active data close to the processor, and the GPU takes
      over highly parallel graphics or numerical workloads that would otherwise slow the CPU down.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-cpu-components-and-memory">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Component</th>
          <th scope="col">Main purpose</th>
          <th scope="col">Why the interaction matters</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Control unit (CU)</td>
          <td>Directs the instruction cycle and sends control signals.</td>
          <td>It coordinates memory access, ALU activity, and data movement on the buses.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Arithmetic logic unit (ALU)</td>
          <td>Performs arithmetic and logical operations.</td>
          <td>It turns decoded instructions into results that can be stored or used immediately.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Registers</td>
          <td>Hold addresses, instructions, data, and intermediate values at very high speed.</td>
          <td>Without registers, the CPU would have to keep fetching tiny pieces of data from slower memory.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Buses</td>
          <td>Carry addresses, data, and control signals between components.</td>
          <td>They are the communication paths that let the processor and memory operate as one system.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      Primary memory supports this cycle in different ways. RAM stores active programs and data, ROM stores
      fixed startup instructions, and cache keeps frequently needed data closer to the CPU. A cache hit
      shortens access time; a cache miss forces the processor to wait for slower memory.
    </p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Memory type</th>
          <th scope="col">Persistence</th>
          <th scope="col">Typical use</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>RAM</td>
          <td>Volatile</td>
          <td>Programs and data currently in use</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>ROM</td>
          <td>Non-volatile</td>
          <td>Firmware and startup instructions</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Cache (L1-L3)</td>
          <td>Volatile</td>
          <td>Frequently reused instructions or data close to the CPU</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Registers</td>
          <td>Volatile</td>
          <td>Immediate values needed inside the current instruction cycle</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      The GPU has a different role. It is designed for workloads where many similar calculations happen at
      the same time, such as image rendering, matrix processing, or video work. The CPU remains the main
      general-purpose processor; the GPU accelerates specific high-throughput tasks.
    </p>

    <h3 id="worked-example-following-one-instruction">Worked example: following one instruction</h3>
    <p>
      Suppose the program counter contains address <code>120</code> and memory address <code>120</code>
      stores the instruction <code>ADD 205</code>.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Stage</th>
          <th scope="col">What happens</th>
          <th scope="col">Main components involved</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Fetch</td>
          <td>The value in the program counter is copied to the memory address register.</td>
          <td>PC, MAR, address bus</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Transfer</td>
          <td>Memory sends the instruction to the memory data register and then to the instruction register.</td>
          <td>MDR, IR, data bus, control bus</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Decode</td>
          <td>The control unit identifies that an addition is required and that the operand is at address 205.</td>
          <td>CU, IR</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Execute</td>
          <td>The operand is fetched and the ALU adds it to the current accumulator value.</td>
          <td>ALU, ACC, MAR, MDR</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Advance</td>
          <td>The program counter updates so the next instruction can be fetched.</td>
          <td>PC</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      A good description does not treat these as isolated facts. It shows the movement of addresses, data,
      and control signals through the cycle.
    </p>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="A1.1.7, A1.1.8, A1.1.9"
    summary="Compare storage media, distinguish lossless from lossy compression, and match cloud services to control requirements."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Secondary storage is non-volatile, so it keeps data when power is removed. The syllabus expects you to
      describe storage categories in terms of purpose and use, not just list device names. The best choice
      depends on speed, capacity, portability, and cost.
    </p>

    <h3 id="applied-in-context-storage-compression-and-cloud">Applied in context</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Technology</th>
          <th scope="col">Best fit</th>
          <th scope="col">Useful distinction</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>SSD</td>
          <td>Fast startup, laptops, applications with frequent reads and writes</td>
          <td>No moving parts; faster access than HDD</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>HDD</td>
          <td>Large, lower-cost storage</td>
          <td>Higher capacity per cost, but slower mechanical access</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Optical / removable flash</td>
          <td>Distribution, transfer, or offline backup</td>
          <td>Portability matters more than peak speed</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Network or cloud storage</td>
          <td>Shared access and remote availability</td>
          <td>Availability depends on network access and provider control</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      Compression reduces file size. Lossless compression preserves all original information, which is why it
      is suitable for text, executable files, and structured data. Lossy compression removes some information
      permanently in exchange for smaller files, which is acceptable only when a small reduction in quality is
      tolerated.
    </p>

    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Method</th>
          <th scope="col">Type</th>
          <th scope="col">Typical use</th>
          <th scope="col">Main trade-off</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Run-length encoding</td>
          <td>Lossless</td>
          <td>Data with repeated values or patterns</td>
          <td>Works poorly when repetition is limited</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Transform coding</td>
          <td>Lossy</td>
          <td>Images, audio, and video</td>
          <td>Smaller files, but the original cannot be reconstructed exactly</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-matching-a-service-model">Worked example: matching a service model</h3>
    <p>
      A school wants to deploy an attendance dashboard. In one case, staff just need to log in and use the
      software. In another, the computing department wants to build and deploy its own custom application. In
      a third, the school wants maximum control over virtual machines and storage.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Need</th>
          <th scope="col">Best cloud model</th>
          <th scope="col">Reason</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Use a ready-made attendance system</td>
          <td>SaaS</td>
          <td>The provider manages the application and infrastructure.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Build and deploy a custom web app</td>
          <td>PaaS</td>
          <td>The platform provides deployment tools without exposing all infrastructure management.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Control servers, operating systems, and storage directly</td>
          <td>IaaS</td>
          <td>The user gains the most control, but also the most responsibility.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="A1.1.3, A1.1.6"
    summary="HL extends the section by comparing CPU and GPU design and by describing pipelining as a throughput strategy in multi-core systems."
    visibility="hl"
  >
    <h3 id="core-understanding-cpu-vs-gpu-and-pipelining">Core understanding</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Feature</th>
          <th scope="col">CPU</th>
          <th scope="col">GPU</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Design focus</td>
          <td>General-purpose control and varied workloads</td>
          <td>Large-scale parallel processing of similar operations</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Typical workload</td>
          <td>Operating systems, process control, mixed application logic</td>
          <td>Graphics rendering, matrix-heavy processing, repeated numeric work</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Strength</td>
          <td>Low-latency decision making</td>
          <td>High throughput across many simultaneous calculations</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      Pipelining is different from adding more cores. It overlaps stages of instruction handling so that one
      instruction can be fetched while another is decoded and another is executed. The goal is higher
      throughput, not magical reduction in the time needed for one individual instruction.
    </p>

    <h3 id="worked-trace-pipeline-overlap">Worked trace: pipeline overlap</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Cycle</th>
          <th scope="col">Instruction 1</th>
          <th scope="col">Instruction 2</th>
          <th scope="col">Instruction 3</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>1</td>
          <td>Fetch</td>
          <td></td>
          <td></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>2</td>
          <td>Decode</td>
          <td>Fetch</td>
          <td></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>3</td>
          <td>Execute</td>
          <td>Decode</td>
          <td>Fetch</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>4</td>
          <td></td>
          <td>Execute</td>
          <td>Decode</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>5</td>
          <td></td>
          <td></td>
          <td>Execute</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <TextbookCallout tone="misconception" label="Common misconception">
      <p>
        More cores do not guarantee a proportional speed increase. Performance still depends on whether the
        workload can be split effectively and whether memory access or other bottlenecks limit throughput.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>
  </TextbookChunk>
</TextbookSectionBlock>

<TextbookSectionBlock section={a12}>
  <TextbookChunk
    label="A1.2.1, A1.2.2"
    summary="Represent values in binary and hexadecimal and explain how different media types are stored as binary."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Binary is the internal language of digital systems. Hexadecimal matters because it gives humans a more
      compact way to inspect the same binary patterns. A precise description links representation to storage:
      computers do not store numbers, characters, images, and audio using different physical principles;
      they store all of them as binary patterns interpreted according to rules.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-binary-representation-and-storage">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Data type</th>
          <th scope="col">Binary idea</th>
          <th scope="col">What changes between examples</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Integer</td>
          <td>Value stored as a binary number</td>
          <td>Range depends on the number of bits available</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Character / text</td>
          <td>Character code stored as binary</td>
          <td>The coding scheme maps symbols to bit patterns</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Image</td>
          <td>Pixels stored as binary colour values</td>
          <td>Resolution and colour depth determine storage size</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Audio / video</td>
          <td>Samples or frames stored as binary sequences</td>
          <td>Sampling, resolution, and compression affect size and quality</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-converting-between-binary-and-hex">Worked example: converting between binary and hex</h3>
    <p>
      Convert <code>1110 1100</code> to hexadecimal by grouping the bits into nibbles and converting each
      nibble separately.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Binary nibble</th>
          <th scope="col">Decimal value</th>
          <th scope="col">Hex digit</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>1110</code></td>
          <td>14</td>
          <td>E</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>1100</code></td>
          <td>12</td>
          <td>C</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The final answer is <code>EC</code>. The value is unchanged; only the representation has become more
      compact for human reading.
    </p>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="A1.2.3, A1.2.4, A1.2.5"
    summary="Use logic gates, truth tables, and logic diagrams to model simple digital decision rules."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      Logic gates implement Boolean decisions in hardware. Truth tables let you analyse every possible input
      combination, and logic diagrams let you construct the physical or conceptual layout of those decisions.
      These three ideas should be treated as connected representations of the same logic.
    </p>

    <h3 id="core-understanding-logic-gates-and-truth-tables">Core understanding</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Gate</th>
          <th scope="col">Meaning</th>
          <th scope="col">Output is 1 when...</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>AND</td>
          <td>All required conditions must be true</td>
          <td>Both inputs are 1</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>OR</td>
          <td>Any acceptable condition may be true</td>
          <td>At least one input is 1</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>NOT</td>
          <td>Reverse the input state</td>
          <td>The input is 0</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>XOR</td>
          <td>Exactly one of two alternatives is true</td>
          <td>The inputs are different</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-building-a-logic-rule">Worked example: building a logic rule</h3>
    <p>
      A security door opens when a valid keycard is present and either the PIN is correct or the door has
      been released remotely.
    </p>
    <p>
      Let <code>K</code> be keycard valid, <code>P</code> be PIN correct, and <code>R</code> be remote
      release. The Boolean expression is <code>K AND (P OR R)</code>.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">K</th>
          <th scope="col">P</th>
          <th scope="col">R</th>
          <th scope="col">Door opens?</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      A valid logic diagram would first combine <code>P</code> and <code>R</code> with an OR gate, then
      combine that output with <code>K</code> using an AND gate.
    </p>

    <TextbookCallout tone="misconception" label="Common misconception">
      <p>
        XOR is not the same as OR. OR allows both inputs to be 1; XOR gives output 1 only when the inputs
        are different.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>
  </TextbookChunk>
</TextbookSectionBlock>

<TextbookSectionBlock section={a13}>
  <TextbookChunk
    label="A1.3.1, A1.3.2, A1.3.3, A1.3.4"
    summary="Describe the operating system as a resource manager, compare scheduling approaches, and evaluate when polling or interrupts are appropriate."
    visibility="sl"
  >
    <p>
      An operating system abstracts hardware complexity so that applications and users do not have to manage
      the CPU, memory, devices, and files directly. Strong answers explain the operating system as a control
      layer that allocates resources and keeps the system stable while many processes compete for attention.
    </p>

    <h3 id="overview-operating-system-functions">Overview</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Function</th>
          <th scope="col">What the OS does</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it matters</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Memory management</td>
          <td>Allocates and protects memory for running processes</td>
          <td>Stops processes interfering with each other</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>File management</td>
          <td>Organizes files, permissions, and storage access</td>
          <td>Keeps data persistent and structured</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Device management</td>
          <td>Coordinates input and output devices</td>
          <td>Lets hardware interact through a consistent system layer</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Scheduling</td>
          <td>Decides which process gets CPU time and when</td>
          <td>Balances responsiveness and throughput</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Security and integrity</td>
          <td>Controls access and background system operations</td>
          <td>Protects the system while services keep running</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      Different scheduling methods fit different priorities. First-come first-served is easy to reason about
      but can delay short jobs behind long ones. Round robin improves fairness for interactive workloads by
      rotating CPU time. Priority scheduling helps urgent tasks, but if it is poorly managed it can lead to
      starvation.
    </p>

    <h3 id="worked-example-polling-vs-interrupts">Worked example: polling or interrupts?</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Situation</th>
          <th scope="col">Better approach</th>
          <th scope="col">Reason</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Keyboard input on a laptop</td>
          <td>Interrupts</td>
          <td>User events are unpredictable and quick response is needed with low power waste.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Temperature check every 10 seconds in a simple building system</td>
          <td>Polling</td>
          <td>The event is regular and controlled latency is acceptable.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Network data arriving at irregular times</td>
          <td>Interrupts</td>
          <td>Waiting for a poll cycle could delay handling and waste CPU cycles.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <TextbookCallout tone="warning" label="Evaluation rule">
      <p>
        Do not treat interrupts as automatically better. The syllabus expects evaluation against context:
        event frequency, overhead, power source, predictability, and required response behaviour.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>
  </TextbookChunk>

  <TextbookChunk
    label="A1.3.5, A1.3.6, A1.3.7"
    summary="HL extends OS study into multitasking risks and introduces control-system components, feedback, and real-world applications."
    visibility="hl"
  >
    <p>
      Higher level work moves from naming operating-system functions to explaining what can go wrong when
      many tasks compete for shared resources. It also extends the section into control systems, where
      computing decisions influence physical processes through sensors and actuators.
    </p>

    <h3 id="core-understanding-multitasking-and-control-systems">Core understanding</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Concept</th>
          <th scope="col">Meaning</th>
          <th scope="col">Why it matters</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Resource contention</td>
          <td>Processes compete for the same CPU time, memory, or device access.</td>
          <td>Poor control causes delays and unstable performance.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Deadlock</td>
          <td>Processes wait on each other in a cycle and cannot progress.</td>
          <td>The system may appear active, but useful work stops.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Open-loop control</td>
          <td>The system acts without measuring the result.</td>
          <td>It is simpler, but it cannot self-correct.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Closed-loop control</td>
          <td>The output is measured and fed back into the controller.</td>
          <td>The system can correct errors and adapt to changing conditions.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p>
      A control system typically links input, process, output, and feedback. Sensors collect measurements,
      the controller applies a control algorithm, actuators change the physical system, and transducers help
      convert signals into a usable form where needed.
    </p>

    <h3 id="worked-example-thermostat-feedback-loop">Worked example: thermostat feedback loop</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Stage</th>
          <th scope="col">What happens</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Input</td>
          <td>The setpoint is <code>22 C</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Sensor reading</td>
          <td>The room temperature is measured at <code>19 C</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Controller decision</td>
          <td>The controller detects an error and sends a stronger heating command.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Actuator response</td>
          <td>The heating system increases output.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Feedback</td>
          <td>The sensor measures the new temperature and the cycle repeats.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <TextbookCallout tone="recap" label="HL recap">
      <p>
        The common thread in this section is control. The operating system controls access to resources inside
        the computer, while the control system controls behaviour in the external process it manages.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>
  </TextbookChunk>
</TextbookSectionBlock>

<TextbookSectionBlock section={a14}>
  <TextbookChunk
    label="A1.4.1"
    summary="Evaluate interpreters and compilers by comparing translation method, error detection, portability, runtime behaviour, and fit for context."
    visibility="hl"
  >
    <p>
      Translation is not just a technical mechanism; it is a design choice. Evaluation means judging which
      approach is most suitable for a scenario and defending that judgement with clear criteria rather than
      claiming that one translator is always best.
    </p>

    <h3 id="core-understanding-translation-criteria">Core understanding</h3>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Criterion</th>
          <th scope="col">Interpreter</th>
          <th scope="col">Compiler</th>
          <th scope="col">Bytecode + JIT context</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Translation process</td>
          <td>Translates and executes during runtime</td>
          <td>Translates the whole program before execution</td>
          <td>Compiles to bytecode, then improves hot paths during runtime</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Error detection</td>
          <td>Errors may appear as the program runs</td>
          <td>Syntax and some semantic issues are caught before execution</td>
          <td>Some checking happens before runtime, with further optimisation while running</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Performance</td>
          <td>Usually slower on repeated execution</td>
          <td>Usually faster once compiled</td>
          <td>Can improve long-run speed after warm-up</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Portability</td>
          <td>High when the interpreter exists on each platform</td>
          <td>Lower unless a separate build is produced per target</td>
          <td>High because the runtime handles platform differences</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3 id="worked-example-choosing-a-translation-approach">Worked example: choosing a translation approach</h3>
    <p>
      Consider three different products:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>A classroom script changed every day and run on mixed operating systems.</li>
      <li>A performance-critical game engine shipped to one known platform.</li>
      <li>A long-running cross-platform service with repeated high-volume workloads.</li>
    </ol>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Scenario</th>
          <th scope="col">Strongest choice</th>
          <th scope="col">Why</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Frequently changed classroom script</td>
          <td>Interpreter</td>
          <td>Fast testing and high portability matter more than peak execution speed.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Performance-critical single-platform game engine</td>
          <td>Compiler</td>
          <td>Precompiled native code supports stronger runtime performance.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Long-running cross-platform service</td>
          <td>Bytecode runtime with JIT</td>
          <td>It balances portability with runtime optimisation for heavily reused code paths.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <TextbookCallout tone="misconception" label="Evaluation warning">
      <p>
        Compiled does not mean universally better. The command term is <strong>evaluate</strong>, so a strong
        answer must weigh the benefits and limitations of each approach against a concrete use case.
      </p>
    </TextbookCallout>
  </TextbookChunk>
